<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_faraday" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Faraday's Law</title>
		
    <corollary xml:id="vid-faraday">
	<title>Faraday's Law</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="8LV-H9LZN8I"><description>Introducing Faraday's Law.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	
	<definition>
		<title>Faraday's Law</title>
		<statement>
      <p>
        
      </p>
			<p><term>Faraday's Law</term> relates the induced electric field around a closed loop to the change in the magnetic flux through that loop:
        <me> \oint \vec{E} \cdot \vec{dl} = - \frac{\partial \Phi_B}{\partial t}</me></p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>The line integral <m>\mathcal{E} = \oint \vec{E} \cdot \vec{dl}</m> is an electromotive force (emf).</alert>  In most situations, this may be effectively treated as a nonzero voltage difference around a closed loop.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert><xref ref="loop-rule"/> is no longer true</alert> unless the change in flux through the loop is zero.  If the flux is not zero, then <m>\sum V = - \frac{\partial \Phi_B}{\partial t}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>The induced electric field can cause current to flow in a closed circuit!</alert> The direction of the current may be found using <xref ref="p_lenz"/>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>The exact electric field can be hard to calculate,</alert> but the value of <m>\mathcal{E}</m> is typically more straightforward.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <figure xml:id="fig_InducedE">
			<title>Induced Electric Field Figure</title>
    		<caption><alert>Example:</alert> vector map for the induced electric field created by a changing magnetic field.</caption>
    		<image source="InducedE.png" width = "50%" ><description>Two gray circles centered on a circle with a cross representing a magnetic field directed into the screen.  On each circle are eight vectors that each point in a straight line tangent to the circle in a counterclockwise direction, representing the counterclocwise electric field.  The vectors on the bigger circle have a smaller length.</description></image>
		</figure>
		</statement>
	</definition>
	



  <conjecture xml:id="rep_mag-induction-table">
<title>Magnetic Induction Table</title>
<statement>
        <p>
          
        </p>
		<p>
			The <xref ref="rep_mag-induction-table-qual"/> may be expanded to include quantitative calculations in addition to keeping track of the qualitative direction or sign.  The previous example is expanded below, assuming the magnetic field has time-varying magnitude <m>B = \alpha t</m> that is uniform over a small circular region <m>A</m>.  Assume a concentric circular wire loop has area <m>A_{loop} \gt A</m> and resistance <m>R</m>.
        </p>

<table>
  <title><alert>Example:</alert> Magnetic Induction Table</title>
  <tabular halign="center">
    <row header="yes" bottom="minor" >
      <cell>Quantity</cell>
      <cell>Direction or Sign</cell>
      <cell>Magnitude</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\vec{A}</m></cell>
      <cell>in</cell>
      <cell><m>A</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\vec{B}</m></cell>
      <cell>in</cell>
      <cell><m>B(t) = \alpha t</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\Phi_B</m></cell>
      <cell><m>+</m></cell>
      <cell><m>\alpha t A</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\frac{d\Phi_B}{dt}</m></cell>
      <cell><m>+</m></cell>
      <cell><m>\alpha A</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\mathcal{E}</m></cell>
      <cell><m>-</m></cell>
      <cell><m>\alpha A</m></cell>
    </row>
		    <row>
      <cell><m>I_{ind}</m></cell>
      <cell>ccw</cell>
      <cell><m>\frac{\alpha A}{R}</m></cell>
    </row>
		<row>
      <cell><m>\vec{\mu}_{ind}</m></cell>
      <cell>out</cell>
      <cell><m>I_{ind} A_{loop}</m></cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>
</table>

</statement>
</conjecture>

